## Layout

- `docs/` holds the pages, and `docs/index.md` is the home page.
- `mkdocs.yml` configures the site and has no `nav:`. The navigation therefore lists a new page or folder in `docs/` automatically, index first and then in alphabetical order.
- `pyproject.toml` and `uv.lock` pin the build tools.
- `site/` holds the build output and is never committed.

## Writing pages

- Before adding a page, search `docs/` for a page that covers the topic. Extend that page instead of adding a second one.
- A page covers one topic. When a page covers a second topic, split it and link the two pages.
- A page starts with one `# Title`. It states what it answers before it gives the detail.
- Related pages share a folder that has its own `index.md`.
- A change that renames or removes a page also fixes every link to that page.
- A page states only verified facts. A fact that can become outdated links its source and names the version or date it describes.

## Markdown rules

- MkDocs renders Python-Markdown, not GitHub Markdown.
- A link between pages has the form `other-page.md`, never `./other-page` or a full URL. `uv run mkdocs build --strict` (the strict build) checks only `.md` links.
- A blank line precedes every list. Without it, the list merges into the preceding paragraph.
- Sub-lists, and paragraphs inside a list item, are indented by 4 spaces.
- No line starts with `#` followed by a digit, as an issue number does. Such a line renders as a heading.

## Verification

- The strict build must pass. The pre-commit hook runs it, so a broken link fails the commit.
- `uv run mkdocs serve` previews the site at <http://127.0.0.1:8000>.
- Do not bypass the hooks. Fix what they report.
{%- if ci_provider == "forgejo" and publish_docs %}

## Publishing

- A push to `main` that changes the documentation publishes the site to <https://{{ docs_host }}/{{ docs_tenant }}/>. Such a push is outward-facing.
- Everything in `docs/` is published. It therefore never holds the following (secrets, tokens, personal data, raw captures).
