## API client

- This project is a Python client for an HTTP API. A caller builds one `Client` and calls one method per endpoint.
- `DEFAULT_BASE_URL` in `{{ pkg_name }}/settings.py` and the `Authorization` header in `{{ pkg_name }}/client.py` are placeholders. Replace both with the values of the API before adding an endpoint.
{%- if spec_first %}

### Spec-first

- `openapi.yaml`, at the repository root, is the canonical description of the API. The client is derived from it and never the reverse. A change in how the client talks to the API starts as a change to that file.
- A repository without `openapi.yaml` is not initialised. Do not add an endpoint to it. Tell the user, and follow `.claude/skills/init-sdk/SKILL.md` when the user asks for the initialisation.
- `uv run python -m tools.openapi_agents` writes `openapi.yaml`, or updates the one that exists. A run calls a paid model through OpenRouter. Start one only when the user asks for it.
- The user reviews a new or updated `openapi.yaml` before it is committed.
- The client takes its public names from `openapi.yaml`: a method from each operationId, a resource from each tag and a model from each schema name. An update of the document therefore keeps those names.
- `uv run datamodel-codegen` generates `{{ pkg_name }}/models.py` from `openapi.yaml`. That file is never edited by hand. It is regenerated after every change to `openapi.yaml` and committed with it, and a hook fails the commit when the two do not match.
- `upstream/` holds the vendored sources that the agents read. A source is replaced by a newer copy and never edited.
- `tools/openapi_agents/project.py` is the only module that knows this API. The other modules of that package come from the project template, and `copier update` updates them. A fix to one of them belongs in the template.
{%- endif %}

### Layout

- `{{ pkg_name }}/client.py` holds `Client`. Every request goes through `Client.request`.
- `{{ pkg_name }}/errors.py` holds every exception the package raises.
- `{{ pkg_name }}/settings.py` holds `Settings`, which reads the environment and `.env`.
- `{{ pkg_name }}/__main__.py` holds the demo that `python -m {{ pkg_name }}` runs. It sends one GET request through `Client.from_env` and prints the response.
- `.env.example` lists the variables that `Settings` reads. `.env` holds the real values and is never committed.
{%- if spec_first %}
- `tools/openapi_agents/` holds the agents that write `openapi.yaml`. It is development tooling and is not part of the package.
- `tools/har_digest/` writes `upstream/` from HAR captures, for an API that has no other source. Its `Recorder` writes a capture from what the client reads. It is development tooling too.
{%- endif %}

### Adding an endpoint

- An endpoint is one public method. The endpoints of one resource share a module, and the client exposes that resource as an attribute.
{%- if spec_first %}
- The client gains an endpoint only after `openapi.yaml` describes it. Its models come from `{{ pkg_name }}/models.py`.
{%- endif %}
- A pydantic model represents each request body and each response body.
- A response model ignores unknown fields, which is the pydantic default. An API adds fields without notice, so `extra="forbid"` would break existing callers.
- A method validates the response into its model. When validation fails, it raises `UnexpectedResponseError`.
- A paginated endpoint is exposed as an iterator that requests one page at a time.
- The test of an endpoint is written before its method.

### Rules

- `httpx2` sends every request, and only inside `Client.request`.
- The client is synchronous. It never retries on its own, so the caller decides when to retry.
- Every exception the package raises derives from `{{ error_class }}`. No `httpx2` or `pydantic` exception reaches the caller.
- The package logs under the `{{ pkg_name }}` logger and attaches only a `NullHandler`. No log record holds an API key, a password, a query parameter or a request body.
- The constructor of `Client` takes its credentials as arguments. Only `Client.from_env` reads the environment.
- A new setting is added to `Settings` and to `.env.example` in the same change.
- Tests replace the network with `httpx2.MockTransport` and patch no internals of the package. No test sends a request to the real API.
{%- endif %}

## Documentation

- The documentation is an MkDocs site. `docs/` holds the pages, and `mkdocs.yml` configures the site.
- `mkdocs.yml` has no `nav:`, so the navigation lists a new page in `docs/` automatically.
- `docs/reference.md` renders the API reference from the docstrings in `{{ pkg_name }}`. Keep public docstrings accurate. Do not copy them into pages.
- A change that alters behaviour, usage or setup also updates the page that covers it. When no page covers it, the change adds one.
- A link between pages has the form `other-page.md`, never `./other-page` or a full URL. `uv run mkdocs build --strict` (the strict build) checks only `.md` links.
- MkDocs renders Python-Markdown, not GitHub Markdown. A list therefore needs a preceding blank line and its sub-lists a 4-space indent, or they merge into the preceding paragraph.
- The strict build must pass. The pre-commit hook runs it, so a broken link fails the commit.
- `uv run mkdocs serve` previews the site at <http://127.0.0.1:8000>.
{%- if ci_provider == "forgejo" and publish_docs %}
- A push to `main` that changes the documentation publishes the site to <https://{{ docs_host }}/{{ docs_tenant }}/>. Such a push is outward-facing.
- Everything in `docs/` is published. It therefore never holds secrets, tokens or raw captures.
